Glad my old code was helpful. I found a similar order-of-magnitude speedup with Stan, even without trying to optimize the code.

I think when Bob and the Team get the function grammar done, these custom model types will be more transparent, because then we'll be able to post function blocks that contain the tricky increment_log_prob stuff, leaving the model block more standard looking. It'll also reduce typos. Looking for example at implementing zero-inflated Poisson or binomial models (there's an example in the manual, I think), the log_sum_exp stuff is a minefield for novices.

Anyway, thanks as always to Bob and the Team. Stan makes every day better.

On Monday, March 31, 2014 8:34:17 PM UTC-7, Nathan Lemoine wrote:
> Hi all,
>
> I saw an older post from Richard McElreath about a working hurdle model in STAN and about the great increase in efficiency compared to JAGS. I just wanted to follow up with my own gamma hurdle model including group-level predictors that works great, gives almost (but not quite) identical inference from JAGS with chains that mix much better. JAGS took me 50,000 burn-ins with 50,000 posterior draws, thinning by every 500th sample, which amounts to about 8 hours running four parallel cores to get even reasonable mixing (but not all parameters mixed well). High autocorrelations would make the chains get stuck in weird parts of the posterior distribution.
>
> In comparison, PySTAN, which automatically runs in parallel, compiled, sampled, and mixed four chains (4,000 burn in and samples, thinning every 20th) in around 10 minutes. The speed increase is incredible, mostly due to the fact that the chains mix so much better that I can reduce the number of burn-ins and posterior draws considerably. Another benefit is that JAGS required me to used scaled parameters to get even close to good mixing, whereas STAN didn't require that, so the model code is much easier to read.
>
> Anyway, here's the model, in case someone else wants to do something similar (hope I did it correctly):
