From u8b81798616f7af4b at groups.google.com  Tue Apr  1 23:22:42 2014
From: u8b81798616f7af4b at groups.google.com (Andrew Ellis)
Date: Tue, 01 Apr 2014 23:22:42 +0000
Subject: Hierarchical regression with a within-subjects factor
Message-ID: <yWdFHk1fjqk.p4Wdx8PPepsJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/yWdFHk1fjqk/m/p4Wdx8PPepsJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I recently asked this question on Cross-validated <http://stats.stackexchange.com/questions/92064/within-subjects-model-in-jags-stan>, but I think I'll have more luck here, as it's probably too software-related:

I have data from a psychophysical experiment, in which I tested S subjects in J=2 conditions (within-subjects design). I am trying to model each subject's probability of giving a (binary) response y as a function of a stimulus parameter x; a standard approach is to use the cumulative normal density function as a psychometric function, which means that I'm using a probit regression model.

Each observation is modelled as a mixture of Bernoulli distributions: either the subject does as he/she is supposed to or has an attentional lapse with a certain probability and then has to guess:

for (n in 1:N) {
 y[n] ~ bernoulli((1-lapse[subj[n]]) * Phi(alpha[subj[n], cond[n]] +
 beta[subj[n], cond[n]] * x[n]) +
 lapse[subj[n]] * guess[subj[n]]);
 }

Intercepts and slopes are allowed to vary by condition nested within subject. My aim is to estimate group-level intercept and slope parameters for each condition.

The problem I'm having is how to allow for the fact that alpha and beta are correlated, and, additionally, the parameters for each condition are correlated for each subject, i.e. if subject A has a high alpha in condition 1, then also in condition 2.

Does anyone have any recommendations on the best way to go about this? It's probably quite a simple problem, but it's got me stumped for the moment.

I have included a minimal working model, plus dataset (for 6 subjects) and R code. I deliberately omitted all correlations between parameters, and the model is pretty much a translation from JAGS code, so all the loops are still in there.

Full stan model:
data {
  int<lower=0> N; // number of observations
  int<lower=0> J; // number of conditions
  int<lower=0> S; // number of subjects
  int<lower=0, upper=1> y[N]; // response
  int<lower=1, upper=J> cond[N]; // condition index variable
  int<lower=1, upper=S> subj[N]; // subject index variable
  vector[N] x; // test orientation (covariate)
}

parameters {
  matrix[S, J] alpha; //varying intercept
  matrix<lower=0>[S, J] beta; //varying slope
  vector<lower=0, upper=1.0>[S] lapse; //subject-specific lapse rate
  vector<lower=0, upper=1.0>[S] guess; //subject-specific guessing parameter
  vector[J] alpha_g; // group-level intercept
  vector<lower=0, upper=100>[J] sigma_alpha_g;
  vector[J] beta_g; // group-level slope
  vector<lower=0, upper=100>[J] sigma_beta_g;
}

model {
    for (s in 1:S) { // subject-specific lapse and guessing parameters
        guess[s] ~ beta(1, 1);
        lapse[s] ~ beta(2, 20);
    }

    for (j in 1:J) { // loop over conditions
        for (s in 1:S) { // loop over subjects
            alpha[s, j] ~ normal(alpha_g[j], sigma_alpha_g[j]);
            beta[s, j] ~ normal(beta_g[j], sigma_beta_g[j]);
        }
        alpha_g[j] ~ normal(0, 1e02); // non-informative prior on group-level intercept
        beta_g ~ normal(0, 1e02); // non-informative prior on group-level slope
  }
  for (n in 1:N) {
    y[n] ~ bernoulli((1-lapse[subj[n]])*Phi(alpha[subj[n], cond[n]] +
                     beta[subj[n], cond[n]] * x[n]) +
                     lapse[subj[n]]*guess[subj[n]]);
  }
}

