MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If I understand your model correctly, yrep is not really a parameter but another "y-type" random variable generated with the same theta that is found for "y". Also, T is a quantity of interest, not a part of the model per se. The data is N, K, x, and y and beta is an "unknown" parameter. As  such, perhaps the following model would work for you:

data {
  int<lower=1> K;
  int<lower=1> N;
  matrix[N,K] x;
  int y[N];
}
parameters {

  vector[K] beta;
}
transformed parameters {
  vector[N] theta;

  theta <- exp(x * beta);
}
model {

  y ~ poisson(theta);
  beta ~ normal(0, 1000);
}
generated quantities{
  vector[N] T;
  int yrep[N];

  for (i in 1:N) {

    yrep[i] <- poisson_rng(theta[i]);

    T[i] <- step(yrep[i] - y[i]) - 0.5 * if_else(yrep[i] == y[i], 1, 0);
  }
}

Using some random data
set.seed(875)
DATA <- list(K = 5, N = 10, x = matrix(rnorm(50, 5, 1), ncol = 5), y = sample.int(n = 25, size = 10))

The second model ran a bit faster and did not throw the sampling error as yrep is neither a transformed paramater nor sampled any longer. Also, you now get the Yreps and T's as outputs.

Hope that helps.

