On Apr 9, 2014, at 2:20 PM, Sean O'Riordain <seor...@tcd.ie> wrote:

> Bob,
> The form of the summation would be that I would like to extend the model described in the thread
> https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/stan-users/643pUMefRpw
> to allow for unknown labels, so I have about 1800 times with known labels and then another 8000 times with unknown labels, but they should be "reasonably infer-able"... I hope!  The intention was to say something equivalent to
>     for (i in 1:n) {
>       vector[K] g; // temporary
>       for (k in 1:K) {
>         g[k] <- -d[k]*(t[i] - T[k] - alpha[k]);
>       }
>       y[i] ~ categorical(softmax(g));
>     }
> where this time the d and alpha had been previously inferred, t is known data and y is an unknown label - possibly a vector/simplex of 21 probabilities of a label - that's where I had intended trying to go.

Neat.  I've been applying some similar models for user-annotated
classification data.

In terms of implementation, I think it's simpler to replace
the above loop with:

  for (n in 1:N)
    y[n] ~ categorical_logit(d * (alpha + T - t[n]));

You just need to make sure each of d, t[n], T, and alpha is declared
as either a vector or a scalar.  The point is to cut down on the number
of operands that get applied (including negation).

Given that T and alpha don't depend on the loop, it'd be be faster,
though more cumbersome, to do this:

  {
     real alpha_plus_T;
     alpha_plus_T <- alpha + T;
     for (n in 1:N)
       y[n] ~ categorical_logit(d * (alpha_plus_T - t[n]));
  }

- Bob

- Bob

