I'm experimenting with modeling correlation / covariance matrices with LKJ priors and it seems like a cool approach. But I'm encountering trouble when I try to scale up to higher dimensionality. In particular, even if I could get the model to run, the K-choose-2 factors will be increasingly hard to estimate as K gets large.

One approach I was thinking of is using a low-rank approximation, perhaps a truncated Cholesky decomposition. Has anyone tried this? What might be a good way to implement it in Stan?

(Why am I using huge correlation matrices? I'm actually trying to adapt a model that's expressed in terms of the _kernel_ between items, so its dimensionality grows with the number of items.)

I tried the naive thing in Stan first, just to see if it happened to work anyway. Here's a model that fails:

pystan.stan(model_code='''
data {
  int<lower=1> n;
  real eta;
}
parameters {
  corr_matrix[n] Sigma;
}
model {
  Sigma ~ lkj_corr(eta);
}
''', data=dict(n=50, eta=1.0))

And it fails with:

Error in function stan::prob::lkj_corr_log(N4stan5agrad3varE): yy is not positive definite. y(0,0) is 1:0.Rejecting proposed initial value with zero density.
Initialization failed after 100 attempts.  Try specifying initial values, reducing ranges of constrained values, or reparameterizing the model.

Thanks,
-Ken

From u743bbacc59f4b25f at groups.google.com  Wed Apr  9 19:41:53 2014
From: u743bbacc59f4b25f at groups.google.com (Ben Goodrich)
Date: Wed, 09 Apr 2014 19:41:53 +0000
Subject: Re: Large correlation matrices and the lkj_corr prior
Message-ID: <YlwuJgCmLxk.2VoGOKkfRkIJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <YlwuJgCmLxk.xjo4dvOFH2IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <YlwuJgCmLxk.xjo4dvOFH2IJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/YlwuJgCmLxk/m/2VoGOKkfRkIJ>
