Thanks for the tips. I applied the vectorization tweak you suggested and got a 30% speedup! ... Then I applied the Matt trick and got a 3x slowdown :Op  The version that employs the Matt trick does however get a better effective sample size, so I think I'm going to stick with it. (The gist linked previously has been updated to this version).

I don't quite understand your other suggestions however. The "rho" variable is a function of both the "logitRho" variable and the "logitRhoEffect" variable, so I don't see a shortcut to computing log(rho). Am I missing something?

Also, I believe that my typical data satisfies the 2*N<<L criterion, but I'm not sure what you mean by "precomputing values and just indexing them when looping over L"; would it be too much trouble to request a toy example? Or is there an example from the manual for this?

Thanks truly,

Mike

--
Mike Lawrence
Graduate Student
Department of Psychology & Neuroscience
Dalhousie University

~ Certainty is (possibly) folly ~

